#
# Run by the Selkies window manager once it is up, as the session's
# application, installed as /etc/xdg/openbox/autostart, which both backends
# read: LightCraft, on the library of the session's project folder
# (`craft/project.sh`), the folder a PhotoCraft or FilmCraft engine of the same
# project opens in.
#
# Everything a person's work is made of stays in that folder, so the next
# session finds it:
#
# - The library, `lightcraft-library`: the catalog (`catalog.snap` and the
#   journal `catalog.log`, which LightCraft appends and syncs before it answers
#   each change, so nothing waits for a Save), with every photo's develop
#   settings, history, versions, masks, ratings, and albums; the user's develop
#   presets (`presets.json`) and the saved export, curve, and filter presets
#   (`prefs.json`); and the preview cache. LightCraft is non-destructive: an
#   edit is a catalog record, never a change to the photo. A new library
#   starts empty (`--no-demo`), without LightCraft's 24 procedural demo photos,
#   which would sit among a person's own.
# - The settings folder, `.lightcraft` (LIGHTCRAFT_CONFIG_DIR): the interface's
#   state (`ui.json`), the lens database once downloaded, optional models, and
#   the logs.
#
# Photos are imported in place (`library.import`, mode `add`) from wherever
# they are in the project folder, so the originals stay where the project's
# other engines read them; the library holds their paths, which are the same in
# every session since the workspace is mounted at one path.
#
# The control channel listens on the Sandbox's loopback only, on
# LIGHTCRAFT_CONTROL_PORT, which the image sets. LightCraft's channel takes no
# token: what guards it is that the port is bound to loopback in a Sandbox that
# runs one person's session and exposes only the Selkies port (sandbox.py).
folder="$(selkies-modal-project)" || exit 1
mkdir -p "${folder}/.lightcraft"
cd "${folder}" || exit 1
export LIGHTCRAFT_CONFIG_DIR="${folder}/.lightcraft"
exec lightcraft --no-demo --library "${folder}/lightcraft-library"
